# The simplest useful shape of a branching dialogue: a hub node that offers topics, one node per topic that
# returns to the hub, and a farewell. One touch of state at the end: an option that only appears once every
# topic has been heard, using visited().
# The same dialogue as a Node Editor graph: tools/nodeeditor/examples/Lore_Keeper.json.

npc: Kweebec_Elder
speaker: Elder
title: The Lore Keeper

== start
Sit a while, {player}. My roots are old and my memory is long. What would you hear of?
-> Tell me about the village.
    <<jump village>>
-> What are the ruins to the north?
    <<jump ruins>>
-> Who fought the old war?
    <<jump war>>
-> What should I do now? <<show if visited("village") and visited("ruins") and visited("war")>>
    <<jump counsel>>
-> That is enough for today.
    <<jump farewell>>

== village
The Kweebec raised these terraces when the river still ran high. Every stone was carried by hand.
We farm what the slopes allow and trade the rest. The merchant by the well will tell you the prices.
<<jump start>>

== ruins
Older than us. Older than the Trork, whatever they claim.
The walls hum at night. Nobody who went looking for the reason came back to explain it.
<<jump start>>

== war
The Trork came down the pass in numbers we had never seen. We held the bridge for nine days.  => And then?
On the tenth the river rose and took the bridge, and them with it. Some say the grove asked it to.
<<jump start>>

== counsel
You have heard the three tales, so you know what matters here: the river, the ruins, and the pass.
Keep to the terraces by day. If the walls hum, walk away. And if the Trork come again, find me first.
<<jump start>>

== farewell
Go well, {player}. The roots remember those who listen.
<<end>>
